1
00:00:00,966 --> 00:00:03,633
Chapter XLVI.

2
00:00:03,633 --> 00:00:07,032
ELIZABETH had been a good deal disappointed in not finding a letter from Jane on their

3
00:00:07,032 --> 00:00:11,565
first arrival at Lambton; and this disappointment had been renewed on each of the mornings that

4
00:00:11,565 --> 00:00:17,032
had now been spent there; but on the third her repining was over, and her sister justified,

5
00:00:17,032 --> 00:00:22,099
by the receipt of two letters from her at once, on one of which was marked that it had

6
00:00:22,099 --> 00:00:23,366
been mis-sent elsewhere.

7
00:00:23,366 --> 00:00:28,599
Elizabeth was not surprised at it, as Jane had written the direction remarkably ill.

8
00:00:28,599 --> 00:00:32,966
They had just been preparing to walk as the letters came in; and her uncle and aunt, leaving

9
00:00:32,966 --> 00:00:36,400
her to enjoy them in quiet, set off by themselves.

10
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:41,166
The one mis-sent must be first attended to; it had been written five days ago.

11
00:00:41,166 --> 00:00:44,666
The beginning contained an account of all their little parties and engagements, with

12
00:00:44,666 --> 00:00:49,433
such news as the country afforded; but the latter half, which was dated a day later,

13
00:00:49,433 --> 00:00:53,532
and written in evident agitation, gave more important intelligence.

14
00:00:53,532 --> 00:00:57,933
It was to this effect:— “Since writing the above, dearest Lizzy,

15
00:00:57,933 --> 00:01:03,099
something has occurred of a most unexpected and serious nature; but I am afraid of alarming

16
00:01:03,099 --> 00:01:05,500
you—be assured that we are all well.

17
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,032
What I have to say relates to poor Lydia.

18
00:01:08,032 --> 00:01:13,933
An express came at twelve last night, just as we were all gone to bed, from Colonel Forster,

19
00:01:13,933 --> 00:01:19,065
to inform us that she was gone off to Scotland with one of his officers; to own the truth,

20
00:01:19,065 --> 00:01:20,065
with Wickham!

21
00:01:20,065 --> 00:01:21,099
Imagine our surprise.

22
00:01:21,099 --> 00:01:25,299
To Kitty, however, it does not seem so wholly unexpected.

23
00:01:25,299 --> 00:01:27,799
I am very, very sorry.

24
00:01:27,799 --> 00:01:29,866
So imprudent a match on both sides!

25
00:01:29,866 --> 00:01:35,466
But I am willing to hope the best, and that his character has been misunderstood.

26
00:01:35,466 --> 00:01:41,065
Thoughtless and indiscreet I can easily believe him, but this step (and let us rejoice over

27
00:01:41,065 --> 00:01:43,299
it) marks nothing bad at heart.

28
00:01:43,299 --> 00:01:47,765
His choice is disinterested at least, for he must know my father can give her nothing.

29
00:01:47,765 --> 00:01:49,966
Our poor mother is sadly grieved.

30
00:01:49,966 --> 00:01:51,832
My father bears it better.

31
00:01:51,832 --> 00:01:55,599
How thankful am I, that we never let them know what has been said against him; we must

32
00:01:55,599 --> 00:01:57,466
forget it ourselves.

33
00:01:57,466 --> 00:02:01,532
They were off Saturday night about twelve, as is conjectured, but were not missed till

34
00:02:01,532 --> 00:02:03,466
yesterday morning at eight.

35
00:02:03,466 --> 00:02:05,866
The express was sent off directly.

36
00:02:05,866 --> 00:02:09,500
My dear Lizzy, they must have passed within ten miles of us.

37
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:12,733
Colonel Forster gives us reason to expect him here soon.

38
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:16,433
Lydia left a few lines for his wife, informing her of their intention.

39
00:02:16,433 --> 00:02:19,700
I must conclude, for I cannot be long from my poor mother.

40
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:24,666
I am afraid you will not be able to make it out, but I hardly know what I have written.”

41
00:02:24,666 --> 00:02:29,532
Without allowing herself time for consideration, and scarcely knowing what she felt, Elizabeth,

42
00:02:29,532 --> 00:02:34,032
on finishing this letter, instantly seized the other, and opening it with the utmost

43
00:02:34,032 --> 00:02:39,332
impatience, read as follows: it had been written a day later than the conclusion of the first.

44
00:02:39,332 --> 00:02:44,299
“By this time, my dearest sister, you have received my hurried letter; I wish this may

45
00:02:44,299 --> 00:02:49,565
be more intelligible, but though not confined for time, my head is so bewildered that I

46
00:02:49,565 --> 00:02:51,866
cannot answer for being coherent.

47
00:02:51,866 --> 00:02:56,966
Dearest Lizzy, I hardly know what I would write, but I have bad news for you, and it

48
00:02:56,966 --> 00:02:59,099
cannot be delayed.

49
00:02:59,099 --> 00:03:03,032
Imprudent as a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now anxious

50
00:03:03,032 --> 00:03:07,599
to be assured it has taken place, for there is but too much reason to fear they are not

51
00:03:07,599 --> 00:03:09,200
gone to Scotland.

52
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:13,900
Colonel Forster came yesterday, having left Brighton the day before, not many hours after

53
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:14,900
the express.

54
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:18,933
Though Lydia’s short letter to Mrs. F. gave them to understand that they were going to

55
00:03:18,933 --> 00:03:23,433
Gretna Green, something was dropped by Denny expressing his belief that W. never intended

56
00:03:23,433 --> 00:03:28,265
to go there, or to marry Lydia at all, which was repeated to Colonel F., who, instantly

57
00:03:28,265 --> 00:03:31,765
taking the alarm, set off from B., intending to trace their route.

58
00:03:31,765 --> 00:03:36,733
He did trace them easily to Clapham, but no farther; for on entering that place, they

59
00:03:36,733 --> 00:03:41,099
removed into a hackney-coach, and dismissed the chaise that brought them from Epsom.

60
00:03:41,099 --> 00:03:45,565
All that is known after this is, that they were seen to continue the London road.

61
00:03:45,565 --> 00:03:47,532
I know not what to think.

62
00:03:47,532 --> 00:03:53,133
After making every possible inquiry on that side London, Colonel F. came on into Hertfordshire,

63
00:03:53,133 --> 00:03:57,765
anxiously renewing them at all the turnpikes, and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but

64
00:03:57,765 --> 00:04:01,633
without any success,—no such people had been seen to pass through.

65
00:04:01,633 --> 00:04:06,933
With the kindest concern he came on to Longbourn, and broke his apprehensions to us in a manner

66
00:04:06,933 --> 00:04:08,765
most creditable to his heart.

67
00:04:08,765 --> 00:04:14,733
I am sincerely grieved for him and Mrs. F.; but no one can throw any blame on them.

68
00:04:14,733 --> 00:04:16,966
Our distress, my dear Lizzy, is very great.

69
00:04:16,966 --> 00:04:21,666
My father and mother believe the worst, but I cannot think so ill of him.

70
00:04:21,666 --> 00:04:26,133
Many circumstances might make it more eligible for them to be married privately in town than

71
00:04:26,133 --> 00:04:30,832
to pursue their first plan; and even if he could form such a design against a young woman

72
00:04:30,832 --> 00:04:35,866
of Lydia’s connections, which is not likely, can I suppose her so lost to everything?

73
00:04:35,866 --> 00:04:36,866
Impossible!

74
00:04:36,866 --> 00:04:41,832
I grieve to find, however, that Colonel F. is not disposed to depend upon their marriage:

75
00:04:41,832 --> 00:04:46,099
he shook his head when I expressed my hopes, and said he feared W. was not a man to be

76
00:04:46,099 --> 00:04:47,099
trusted.

77
00:04:47,099 --> 00:04:49,966
My poor mother is really ill, and keeps her room.

78
00:04:49,966 --> 00:04:54,299
Could she exert herself, it would be better, but this is not to be expected; and as to

79
00:04:54,299 --> 00:04:57,866
my father, I never in my life saw him so affected.

80
00:04:57,866 --> 00:05:02,233
Poor Kitty has anger for having concealed their attachment; but as it was a matter of

81
00:05:02,233 --> 00:05:03,233
confidence, one cannot wonder.

82
00:05:03,233 --> 00:05:07,900
I am truly glad, dearest Lizzy, that you have been spared something of these distressing

83
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:12,866
scenes; but now, as the first shock is over, shall I own that I long for your return?

84
00:05:12,866 --> 00:05:17,799
I am not so selfish, however, as to press for it, if inconvenient.

85
00:05:17,799 --> 00:05:18,799
Adieu!

86
00:05:18,799 --> 00:05:22,532
I take up my pen again to do, what I have just told you I would not; but circumstances

87
00:05:22,532 --> 00:05:27,166
are such, that I cannot help earnestly begging you all to come here as soon as possible.

88
00:05:27,166 --> 00:05:31,099
I know my dear uncle and aunt so well, that I am not afraid of requesting it, though I

89
00:05:31,099 --> 00:05:33,633
have still something more to ask of the former.

90
00:05:33,633 --> 00:05:37,200
My father is going to London with Colonel Forster instantly, to try to discover her.

91
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:41,366
What he means to do, I am sure I know not; but his excessive distress will not allow

92
00:05:41,366 --> 00:05:45,433
him to pursue any measure in the best and safest way, and Colonel Forster is obliged

93
00:05:45,433 --> 00:05:47,733
to be at Brighton again to-morrow evening.

94
00:05:47,733 --> 00:05:52,099
In such an exigence my uncle’s advice and assistance would be everything in the world;

95
00:05:52,099 --> 00:05:57,233
he will immediately comprehend what I must feel, and I rely upon his goodness.”

96
00:05:57,233 --> 00:05:59,799
“Oh! where, where is my uncle?”

97
00:05:59,799 --> 00:06:03,900
cried Elizabeth, darting from her seat as she finished the letter, in eagerness to follow

98
00:06:03,900 --> 00:06:07,733
him, without losing a moment of the time so precious; but as she reached the door, it

99
00:06:07,733 --> 00:06:11,133
was opened by a servant, and Mr. Darcy appeared.

100
00:06:11,133 --> 00:06:15,466
Her pale face and impetuous manner made him start, and before he could recover himself

101
00:06:15,466 --> 00:06:19,633
enough to speak, she, in whose mind every idea was superseded by Lydia’s situation,

102
00:06:19,633 --> 00:06:23,666
hastily exclaimed, “I beg your pardon, but I must leave you.

103
00:06:23,666 --> 00:06:28,733
I must find Mr. Gardiner this moment on business that cannot be delayed; I have not an instant

104
00:06:28,733 --> 00:06:29,733
to lose.”

105
00:06:29,733 --> 00:06:30,966
“Good God!

106
00:06:30,966 --> 00:06:31,966
what is the matter?”

107
00:06:31,966 --> 00:06:37,032
cried he, with more feeling than politeness; then recollecting himself, “I will not detain

108
00:06:37,032 --> 00:06:41,332
you a minute; but let me, or let the servant, go after Mr. and Mrs. Gardiner.

109
00:06:41,332 --> 00:06:44,466
You are not well enough; you cannot go yourself.”

110
00:06:44,466 --> 00:06:48,233
Elizabeth hesitated; but her knees trembled under her, and she felt how little would be

111
00:06:48,233 --> 00:06:50,700
gained by her attempting to pursue them.

112
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:54,532
Calling back the servant, therefore, she commissioned him, though in so breathless an accent as

113
00:06:54,532 --> 00:06:59,332
made her almost unintelligible, to fetch his master and mistress home instantly.

114
00:06:59,332 --> 00:07:04,000
On his quitting the room, she sat down, unable to support herself, and looking so miserably

115
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,799
ill, that it was impossible for Darcy to leave her, or to refrain from saying, in a tone

116
00:07:07,799 --> 00:07:11,332
of gentleness and commiseration, “Let me call your maid.

117
00:07:11,332 --> 00:07:13,400
Is there nothing you could take to give you present relief?

118
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:16,065
A glass of wine; shall I get you one?

119
00:07:16,065 --> 00:07:17,332
You are very ill.”

120
00:07:17,332 --> 00:07:21,500
“No, I thank you,” she replied, endeavouring to recover herself.

121
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:23,366
“There is nothing the matter with me.

122
00:07:23,366 --> 00:07:28,000
I am quite well, I am only distressed by some dreadful news which I have just received from

123
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,433
Longbourn.”

124
00:07:29,433 --> 00:07:33,265
She burst into tears as she alluded to it, and for a few minutes could not speak another

125
00:07:33,265 --> 00:07:34,265
word.

126
00:07:34,265 --> 00:07:38,133
Darcy, in wretched suspense, could only say something indistinctly of his

127
00:07:39,133 --> 00:07:40,433
“I have not an instant to lose” concern, and observe her in compassionate

128
00:07:40,433 --> 00:07:41,433
silence.

129
00:07:41,433 --> 00:07:43,133
At length she spoke again.

130
00:07:43,133 --> 00:07:47,265
“I have just had a letter from Jane, with such dreadful news.

131
00:07:47,265 --> 00:07:49,400
It cannot be concealed from anyone.

132
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:54,299
My youngest sister has left all her friends—has eloped; has thrown herself into the power

133
00:07:54,299 --> 00:07:56,366
of—of Mr. Wickham.

134
00:07:56,366 --> 00:07:59,532
They are gone off together from Brighton.

135
00:07:59,532 --> 00:08:01,933
You know him too well to doubt the rest.

136
00:08:01,933 --> 00:08:07,532
She has no money, no connections, nothing that can tempt him to—she is lost for ever.”

137
00:08:07,532 --> 00:08:09,832
Darcy was fixed in astonishment.

138
00:08:09,832 --> 00:08:14,900
“When I consider,” she added, in a yet more agitated voice, “that I might have

139
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:16,032
prevented it!

140
00:08:16,032 --> 00:08:17,633
I who knew what he was.

141
00:08:17,633 --> 00:08:23,233
Had I but explained some part of it only—some part of what I learnt, to my own family!

142
00:08:23,233 --> 00:08:25,933
Had his character been known, this could not have happened.

143
00:08:25,933 --> 00:08:29,532
But it is all, all too late now.”

144
00:08:29,532 --> 00:08:34,133
“I am grieved, indeed,” cried Darcy: “grieved—shocked.

145
00:08:34,133 --> 00:08:37,200
But is it certain, absolutely certain?”

146
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:38,299
“Oh, yes!

147
00:08:38,299 --> 00:08:42,200
They left Brighton together on Sunday night, and were traced almost to London, but not

148
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:45,099
beyond: they are certainly not gone to Scotland.”

149
00:08:45,099 --> 00:08:49,000
“And what has been done, what has been attempted, to recover her?”

150
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,966
“My father has gone to London, and Jane has written to beg my uncle’s immediate

151
00:08:52,966 --> 00:08:56,599
assistance, and we shall be off, I hope, in half an hour.

152
00:08:56,599 --> 00:08:59,966
But nothing can be done; I know very well that nothing can be done.

153
00:08:59,966 --> 00:09:02,032
How is such a man to be worked on?

154
00:09:02,032 --> 00:09:03,633
How are they even to be discovered?

155
00:09:03,633 --> 00:09:05,299
I have not the smallest hope.

156
00:09:05,299 --> 00:09:08,565
It is every way horrible!”

157
00:09:08,565 --> 00:09:11,099
Darcy shook his head in silent acquiescence.

158
00:09:11,099 --> 00:09:17,466
“When my eyes were opened to his real character, oh! had I known what I ought, what I dared

159
00:09:17,466 --> 00:09:18,532
to do!

160
00:09:18,532 --> 00:09:21,200
But I knew not—I was afraid of doing too much.

161
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:24,832
Wretched, wretched mistake!”

162
00:09:24,832 --> 00:09:26,400
Darcy made no answer.

163
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:31,332
He seemed scarcely to hear her, and was walking up and down the room in earnest meditation;

164
00:09:31,332 --> 00:09:34,532
his brow contracted, his air gloomy.

165
00:09:34,532 --> 00:09:37,166
Elizabeth soon observed, and instantly understood it.

166
00:09:37,166 --> 00:09:42,532
Her power was sinking; everything must sink under such a proof of family weakness, such

167
00:09:42,532 --> 00:09:45,166
an assurance of the deepest disgrace.

168
00:09:45,166 --> 00:09:49,466
She could neither wonder nor condemn; but the belief of his self-conquest brought nothing

169
00:09:49,466 --> 00:09:53,700
consolatory to her bosom, afforded no palliation of her distress.

170
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:58,233
It was, on the contrary, exactly calculated to make her understand her own wishes; and

171
00:09:58,233 --> 00:10:03,133
never had she so honestly felt that she could have loved him, as now, when all love must

172
00:10:03,133 --> 00:10:04,700
be vain.

173
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:08,466
But self, though it would intrude, could not engross her.

174
00:10:08,466 --> 00:10:13,233
Lydia—the humiliation, the misery she was bringing on them all—soon swallowed up every

175
00:10:13,233 --> 00:10:18,466
private care; and covering her face with her handkerchief, Elizabeth was soon lost to everything

176
00:10:18,466 --> 00:10:23,433
else; and, after a pause of several minutes, was only recalled to a sense of her situation

177
00:10:23,433 --> 00:10:29,299
by the voice of her companion, who, in a manner which, though it spoke compassion, spoke likewise

178
00:10:29,299 --> 00:10:32,633
restraint, said,— “I am afraid you have been long desiring

179
00:10:32,633 --> 00:10:37,200
my absence, nor have I anything to plead in excuse of my stay, but real, though unavailing

180
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:38,200
concern.

181
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:43,065
Would to Heaven that anything could be either said or done on my part, that might offer

182
00:10:43,065 --> 00:10:44,765
consolation to such distress!

183
00:10:44,765 --> 00:10:49,733
But I will not torment you with vain wishes, which may seem purposely to ask for your thanks.

184
00:10:49,733 --> 00:10:54,299
This unfortunate affair will, I fear, prevent my sister’s having the pleasure of seeing

185
00:10:54,299 --> 00:10:55,633
you at Pemberley to-day.”

186
00:10:55,633 --> 00:10:56,633
“Oh, yes!

187
00:10:56,633 --> 00:10:59,332
Be so kind as to apologize for us to Miss Darcy.

188
00:10:59,332 --> 00:11:02,933
Say that urgent business calls us home immediately.

189
00:11:02,933 --> 00:11:05,400
Conceal the unhappy truth as long as it is possible.

190
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:08,166
I know it cannot be long.”

191
00:11:08,166 --> 00:11:13,000
He readily assured her of his secrecy, again expressed his sorrow for her distress, wished

192
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:17,433
it a happier conclusion than there was at present reason to hope, and, leaving his compliments

193
00:11:17,433 --> 00:11:22,832
for her relations, with only one serious parting look, went away.

194
00:11:22,832 --> 00:11:26,866
As he quitted the room, Elizabeth felt how improbable it was that they should ever see

195
00:11:26,866 --> 00:11:32,433
each other again on such terms of cordiality as had marked their several meetings in Derbyshire;

196
00:11:32,433 --> 00:11:36,233
and as she threw a retrospective glance over the whole of their acquaintance, so full of

197
00:11:36,233 --> 00:11:40,700
contradictions and varieties, sighed at the perverseness of those feelings which would

198
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:46,032
now have promoted its continuance, and would formerly have rejoiced in its termination.

199
00:11:46,032 --> 00:11:50,799
If gratitude and esteem are good foundations of affection, Elizabeth’s change of sentiment

200
00:11:50,799 --> 00:11:53,599
will be neither improbable nor faulty.

201
00:11:53,599 --> 00:11:59,000
But if otherwise, if the regard springing from such sources is unreasonable or unnatural,

202
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,733
in comparison of what is so often described as arising on a first interview with its object,

203
00:12:03,733 --> 00:12:08,733
and even before two words have been exchanged, nothing can be said in her defence, except

204
00:12:08,733 --> 00:12:13,065
that she had given somewhat of a trial to the latter method, in her partiality for Wickham,

205
00:12:13,065 --> 00:12:18,233
and that its ill success might, perhaps, authorize her to seek the other less interesting mode

206
00:12:18,233 --> 00:12:19,400
of attachment.

207
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:24,933
Be that as it may, she saw him go with regret; and in this early example of what Lydia’s

208
00:12:24,933 --> 00:12:30,233
infamy must produce, found additional anguish as she reflected on that wretched business.

209
00:12:30,233 --> 00:12:33,933
Never since reading Jane’s second letter had she entertained a hope of Wickham’s

210
00:12:33,933 --> 00:12:35,532
meaning to marry her.

211
00:12:35,532 --> 00:12:40,400
No one but Jane, she thought, could flatter herself with such an expectation.

212
00:12:40,400 --> 00:12:43,032
Surprise was the least of all her feelings on this development.

213
00:12:43,032 --> 00:12:47,832
While the contents of the first letter remained on her mind, she was all surprise, all astonishment,

214
00:12:47,832 --> 00:12:51,599
that Wickham should marry a girl whom it was impossible he could marry for money; and how

215
00:12:51,599 --> 00:12:54,633
Lydia could ever have attached him had appeared incomprehensible.

216
00:12:54,633 --> 00:12:57,765
But now it was all too natural.

217
00:12:57,765 --> 00:13:01,933
For such an attachment as this, she might have sufficient charms; and though she did

218
00:13:01,933 --> 00:13:05,765
not suppose Lydia to be deliberately engaging in an elopement, without the intention of

219
00:13:05,765 --> 00:13:10,166
marriage, she had no difficulty in believing that neither her virtue nor her understanding

220
00:13:10,166 --> 00:13:13,099
would preserve her from falling an easy prey.

221
00:13:13,099 --> 00:13:17,666
She had never perceived, while the regiment was in Hertfordshire, that Lydia had any partiality

222
00:13:17,666 --> 00:13:22,299
for him; but she was convinced that Lydia had wanted only encouragement to attach herself

223
00:13:22,299 --> 00:13:24,000
to anybody.

224
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:27,666
Sometimes one officer, sometimes another, had been her favourite, as their attentions

225
00:13:27,666 --> 00:13:29,500
raised them in her opinion.

226
00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:33,565
Her affections had been continually fluctuating, but never without an object.

227
00:13:33,565 --> 00:13:38,000
The mischief of neglect and mistaken indulgence towards such a girl—oh!

228
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,466
how acutely did she now feel it!

229
00:13:40,466 --> 00:13:45,599
She was wild to be at home—to hear, to see, to be upon the spot to share with Jane in

230
00:13:45,599 --> 00:13:51,332
the cares that must now fall wholly upon her, in a family so deranged; a father absent,

231
00:13:51,332 --> 00:13:56,700
a mother incapable of exertion, and requiring constant attendance; and though almost persuaded

232
00:13:56,700 --> 00:14:01,000
that nothing could be done for Lydia, her uncle’s interference seemed of the utmost

233
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,966
importance, and till he entered the room the misery of her impatience was severe.

234
00:14:04,966 --> 00:14:09,065
Mr. and Mrs. Gardiner had hurried back in alarm, supposing, by the servant’s account,

235
00:14:09,065 --> 00:14:13,900
that their niece was taken suddenly ill; but satisfying them instantly on that head, she

236
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:18,299
eagerly communicated the cause of their summons, reading the two letters aloud, and dwelling

237
00:14:18,299 --> 00:14:20,765
on the postscript of the last with trembling energy.

238
00:14:20,765 --> 00:14:24,366
Though Lydia had never been a favourite with them, Mr. and Mrs. Gardiner could not but

239
00:14:24,366 --> 00:14:26,332
be deeply affected.

240
00:14:26,332 --> 00:14:31,032
Not Lydia only, but all were concerned in it; and after the first exclamations of surprise

241
00:14:31,032 --> 00:14:34,733
and horror, Mr. Gardiner readily promised every assistance in his power.

242
00:14:34,733 --> 00:14:39,599
Elizabeth, though expecting no less, thanked him with tears of gratitude; and all three

243
00:14:39,599 --> 00:14:44,733
being actuated by one spirit, everything relating to their journey was speedily settled.

244
00:14:44,733 --> 00:14:46,599
They were to be off as soon as possible.

245
00:14:46,599 --> 00:14:49,332
“But what is to be done about Pemberley?”

246
00:14:49,332 --> 00:14:50,332
cried Mrs. Gardiner.

247
00:14:50,332 --> 00:14:55,065
“John told us Mr. Darcy was here when you sent for us;—was it so?”

248
00:14:55,065 --> 00:14:58,532
“Yes; and I told him we should not be able to keep our engagement.

249
00:14:58,532 --> 00:14:59,900
That is all settled.”

250
00:14:59,900 --> 00:15:01,765
“What is all settled?”

251
00:15:01,765 --> 00:15:04,032
repeated the other, as she ran into her room to prepare.

252
00:15:04,032 --> 00:15:07,866
“And are they upon such terms as for her to disclose the real truth?

253
00:15:07,866 --> 00:15:10,700
Oh, that I knew how it was!”

254
00:15:10,700 --> 00:15:15,065
But wishes were vain; or, at best, could serve only to amuse her in the hurry and confusion

255
00:15:15,065 --> 00:15:16,700
of the following hour.

256
00:15:16,700 --> 00:15:20,933
Had Elizabeth been at leisure to be idle, she would have remained certain that all employment

257
00:15:20,933 --> 00:15:25,366
was impossible to one so wretched as herself; but she had her share of business as well

258
00:15:25,366 --> 00:15:29,166
as her aunt, and amongst the rest there were notes to be written to all their friends at

259
00:15:29,166 --> 00:15:32,966
Lambton, with false excuses for their sudden departure.

260
00:15:32,966 --> 00:15:36,866
An hour, however, saw the whole completed; and Mr. Gardiner, meanwhile, having settled

261
00:15:36,866 --> 00:15:42,065
his account at the inn, nothing remained to be done but to go; and Elizabeth, after all

262
00:15:42,065 --> 00:15:45,666
the misery of the morning, found herself, in a shorter space of time than she could

263
00:15:45,666 --> 00:15:53,532
have supposed, seated in the carriage, and on the road to Longbourn.

264
00:15:57,799 --> 00:15:59,065
CHAPTER XLVII.

265
00:15:59,065 --> 00:16:04,599
“I HAVE been thinking it over again, Elizabeth,” said her uncle, as they drove from the town;

266
00:16:04,599 --> 00:16:09,799
“and really, upon serious consideration, I am much more inclined than I was to judge

267
00:16:09,799 --> 00:16:12,233
as your eldest sister does of the matter.

268
00:16:12,233 --> 00:16:15,966
It appears to me so very unlikely that any young man should form such a design against

269
00:16:15,966 --> 00:16:21,166
a girl who is by no means unprotected or friendless, and who was actually staying in his Colonel’s

270
00:16:21,166 --> 00:16:24,400
family, that I am strongly inclined to hope the best.

271
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:26,966
Could he expect that her friends would not step forward?

272
00:16:26,966 --> 00:16:30,332
Could he expect to be noticed again by the regiment, after such an affront to Colonel

273
00:16:30,332 --> 00:16:31,433
Forster?

274
00:16:31,433 --> 00:16:33,933
His temptation is not adequate to the risk.”

275
00:16:33,933 --> 00:16:35,799
“Do you really think so?”

276
00:16:35,799 --> 00:16:38,500
cried Elizabeth, brightening up for a moment.

277
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:43,400
“Upon my word,” said Mrs. Gardiner, “I begin to be of your uncle’s opinion.

278
00:16:43,400 --> 00:16:47,733
It is really too great a violation of decency, honour, and interest, for him to be guilty

279
00:16:47,733 --> 00:16:48,733
of it.

280
00:16:48,733 --> 00:16:50,900
I cannot think so very ill of Wickham.

281
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:54,832
Can you, yourself, Lizzie, so wholly give him up, as to believe him capable of it?”

282
00:16:54,832 --> 00:16:57,832
“Not perhaps of neglecting his own interest.

283
00:16:57,832 --> 00:17:00,599
But of every other neglect I can believe him capable.

284
00:17:00,599 --> 00:17:02,832
If, indeed, it should be so!

285
00:17:02,832 --> 00:17:05,099
But I dare not hope it.

286
00:17:05,099 --> 00:17:08,966
Why should they not go on to Scotland, if that had been the case?”

287
00:17:08,966 --> 00:17:12,866
“In the first place,” replied Mr. Gardiner, “there is no absolute proof that they are

288
00:17:12,866 --> 00:17:14,299
not gone to Scotland.”

289
00:17:14,299 --> 00:17:18,299
“Oh, but their removing from the chaise into a hackney coach is such a presumption!

290
00:17:18,299 --> 00:17:23,099
And, besides, no traces of them were to be found on the Barnet road.”

291
00:17:23,099 --> 00:17:27,900
“Well, then,—supposing them to be in London—they may be there, though for the purpose of concealment,

292
00:17:27,900 --> 00:17:30,000
for no more exceptionable purpose.

293
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:34,265
It is not likely that money should be very abundant on either side; and it might strike

294
00:17:34,265 --> 00:17:37,466
them that they could be more economically, though less expeditiously, married in London,

295
00:17:37,466 --> 00:17:38,765
than in Scotland.”

296
00:17:38,765 --> 00:17:41,765
“But why all this secrecy?

297
00:17:41,765 --> 00:17:44,065
Why any fear of detection?

298
00:17:44,065 --> 00:17:46,133
Why must their marriage be private?

299
00:17:46,133 --> 00:17:49,000
Oh, no, no—this is not likely.

300
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:53,466
His most particular friend, you see by Jane’s account, was persuaded of his never intending

301
00:17:53,466 --> 00:17:54,733
to marry her.

302
00:17:54,733 --> 00:17:57,200
Wickham will never marry a woman without some money.

303
00:17:57,200 --> 00:17:58,799
He cannot afford it.

304
00:17:58,799 --> 00:18:03,933
And what claims has Lydia, what attractions has she beyond youth, health, and good humour,

305
00:18:03,933 --> 00:18:09,265
that could make him for her sake forego every chance of benefiting himself by marrying well?

306
00:18:09,265 --> 00:18:13,765
As to what restraint the apprehensions of disgrace in the corps might throw on a dishonourable

307
00:18:13,765 --> 00:18:18,166
elopement with her, I am not able to judge; for I know nothing of the effects that such

308
00:18:18,166 --> 00:18:19,332
a step might produce.

309
00:18:19,332 --> 00:18:24,265
But as to your other objection, I am afraid it will hardly hold good.

310
00:18:24,265 --> 00:18:28,966
Lydia has no brothers to step forward; and he might imagine, from my father’s behaviour,

311
00:18:28,966 --> 00:18:31,966
from his indolence and the little attention he has ever seemed to give to what was going

312
00:18:31,966 --> 00:18:36,265
forward in his family, that he would do as little and think as little about it, as any

313
00:18:36,265 --> 00:18:38,299
father could do, in such a matter.”

314
00:18:38,299 --> 00:18:42,900
“But can you think that Lydia is so lost to everything but love of him, as to consent

315
00:18:42,900 --> 00:18:45,599
to live with him on any other terms than marriage?”

316
00:18:45,599 --> 00:18:50,832
“It does seem, and it is most shocking, indeed,” replied Elizabeth, with tears in

317
00:18:50,832 --> 00:18:56,265
her eyes, “that a sister’s sense of decency and virtue in such a point should admit of

318
00:18:56,265 --> 00:18:57,265
doubt.

319
00:18:57,265 --> 00:18:59,565
But, really, I know not what to say.

320
00:18:59,565 --> 00:19:01,233
Perhaps I am not doing her justice.

321
00:19:01,233 --> 00:19:06,233
But she is very young: she has never been taught to think on serious subjects; and for

322
00:19:06,233 --> 00:19:11,633
the last half year, nay, for a twelvemonth, she has been given up to nothing but amusement

323
00:19:11,633 --> 00:19:13,032
and vanity.

324
00:19:13,032 --> 00:19:17,332
She has been allowed to dispose of her time in the most idle and frivolous manner, and

325
00:19:17,332 --> 00:19:20,400
to adopt any opinions that came in her way.

326
00:19:20,400 --> 00:19:24,299
Since the ——shire were first quartered in Meryton, nothing but love, flirtation,

327
00:19:24,299 --> 00:19:26,000
and officers, have been in her head.

328
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,733
She has been doing everything in her power, by thinking and talking on the subject, to

329
00:19:29,733 --> 00:19:35,265
give greater—what shall I call it?—susceptibility to her feelings; which are naturally lively

330
00:19:35,265 --> 00:19:36,265
enough.

331
00:19:36,265 --> 00:19:40,700
And we all know that Wickham has every charm of person and address that can captivate a

332
00:19:40,700 --> 00:19:41,700
woman.”

333
00:19:41,700 --> 00:19:46,500
“But you see that Jane,” said her aunt, “does not think so ill of Wickham, as to

334
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:48,400
believe him capable of the attempt.”

335
00:19:48,400 --> 00:19:51,000
“Of whom does Jane ever think ill?

336
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:55,633
And who is there, whatever might be their former conduct, that she would believe capable

337
00:19:55,633 --> 00:19:58,799
of such an attempt, till it were proved against them?

338
00:19:58,799 --> 00:20:02,832
But Jane knows, as well as I do, what Wickham really is.

339
00:20:02,832 --> 00:20:07,265
We both know that he has been profligate in every sense of the word; that he has neither

340
00:20:07,265 --> 00:20:11,700
integrity nor honour; that he is as false and deceitful as he is insinuating.”

341
00:20:11,700 --> 00:20:14,866
“And do you really know all this?”

342
00:20:14,866 --> 00:20:19,133
cried Mrs. Gardiner, whose curiosity as to the mode of her intelligence was all alive.

343
00:20:19,133 --> 00:20:22,233
“I do, indeed,” replied Elizabeth, colouring.

344
00:20:22,233 --> 00:20:28,133
“I told you the other day of his infamous behaviour to Mr. Darcy; and you, yourself,

345
00:20:28,133 --> 00:20:32,565
when last at Longbourn, heard in what manner he spoke of the man who had behaved with such

346
00:20:32,565 --> 00:20:35,633
forbearance and liberality towards him.

347
00:20:35,633 --> 00:20:39,666
And there are other circumstances which I am not at liberty—which it is not worth

348
00:20:39,666 --> 00:20:44,233
while to relate; but his lies about the whole Pemberley family are endless.

349
00:20:44,233 --> 00:20:49,832
From what he said of Miss Darcy, I was thoroughly prepared to see a proud, reserved, disagreeable

350
00:20:49,832 --> 00:20:50,832
girl.

351
00:20:50,832 --> 00:20:52,400
Yet he knew to the contrary himself.

352
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:56,466
He must know that she was as amiable and unpretending as we have found her.”

353
00:20:56,466 --> 00:20:58,832
“But does Lydia know nothing of this?

354
00:20:58,832 --> 00:21:01,900
can she be ignorant of what you and Jane seem so well to understand?”

355
00:21:01,900 --> 00:21:05,866
“Oh, yes!—that, that is the worst of all.

356
00:21:05,866 --> 00:21:10,565
Till I was in Kent, and saw so much both of Mr. Darcy and his relation Colonel Fitzwilliam,

357
00:21:10,565 --> 00:21:13,400
I was ignorant of the truth myself.

358
00:21:13,400 --> 00:21:17,332
And when I returned home the ——shire was to leave Meryton in a week or fortnight’s

359
00:21:17,332 --> 00:21:18,332
time.

360
00:21:18,332 --> 00:21:22,466
As that was the case, neither Jane, to whom I related the whole, nor I, thought it necessary

361
00:21:22,466 --> 00:21:27,166
to make our knowledge public; for of what use could it apparently be to anyone, that

362
00:21:27,166 --> 00:21:31,099
the good opinion, which all the neighbourhood had of him, should then be overthrown?

363
00:21:31,099 --> 00:21:35,265
And even when it was settled that Lydia should go with Mrs. Forster, the necessity of opening

364
00:21:35,265 --> 00:21:37,565
her eyes to his character never occurred to me.

365
00:21:37,565 --> 00:21:41,233
That she could be in any danger from the deception never entered my head.

366
00:21:41,233 --> 00:21:46,000
That such a consequence as this should ensue, you may easily believe was far enough from

367
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:47,200
my thoughts.”

368
00:21:47,200 --> 00:21:51,133
“When they all removed to Brighton, therefore, you had no reason, I suppose, to believe them

369
00:21:51,133 --> 00:21:52,166
fond of each other?”

370
00:21:52,166 --> 00:21:54,299
“Not the slightest.

371
00:21:54,299 --> 00:21:58,433
I can remember no symptom of affection on either side; and had anything of the kind

372
00:21:58,433 --> 00:22:02,599
been perceptible, you must be aware that ours is not a family on which it could be thrown

373
00:22:02,599 --> 00:22:03,599
away.

374
00:22:03,599 --> 00:22:07,151
When first he entered the corps, she was ready enough to admire him; but so we all were.
